\title{Groups: A Categorical View}

\p{The standard interpretation of a group involves a set that is closed under the group operation along with guarantees of associativity, inverses, and an identity element.}
\p{There is, however, another way to look at them. A group can be thought of as a category with a single object and a collection of [endomorphisms](category-theory) corresponding to each group element. That is, for every #{g} in a group #{G}, we have a corresponding morphism in our category}

##{
\qquad
g:\bullet\longrightarrow\bullet
}

\p{}

\p{In this view, group multiplication corresponds to composition of morphisms:}

##{
\bullet
\xrightarrow{\ f\ }
\bullet
\xrightarrow{\ g\ }
\bullet,
\qquad
g\circ f=gf.
}

\p{In fact, we can think of categories that are composed of a single object as monoids (from the Greek word "mono", meaning "single" or "one"). Groups are monoids where each morphism has an inverse.}
\p{}
\p{So far, we've been looking at a category corresponding to a single group. But we can build a category consisting of all possible groups, where each object in the category represents an individual group.}
\p{What about the morphisms in this grand category?}
\p{In this case, the morphisms represent group homomorphisms! Recall that group homomorphisms preserve group operations. By that I mean, given groups #{G} and #{H}, and a group homomorphism #{\varphi:G\to H}, we have}

##{
\varphi(xy) = \varphi(x)\varphi(y) \quad \forall x, y \in G
}

\p{If the morphism is invertible, then #{G} and #{H} are isomorphic. That is, as close to equality as you can get in a categorical setting.}
